...

> Tru64 UNIX will be fault tolerant long before VMS.

Given that VMS *was* fault-tolerant over a decade ago on ftVAX, that
statement seems at least a bit excessive.

 Can you say process
> pairing and process mirroring? Sure you can, if you work for Tru64!

And of course you could have 'said it' for more than 2 decades on NSK, where
it was pioneered (unless IBM got there on some platform even earlier, as is
so often the case).

But process-pairing is anything but transparent to the code (which is why
hardware approaches are so much more common):  Jim Gray observed (in his
excellent tome on TP with Andreas Reuter) that correct process-pair coding
was some of the most complex one could find, so it's not exactly a panacea,
and certainly not the easiest answer for most FT use (given the relative
costs of hardware - even FT hardware, which is rapidly becoming available in
the 'industry-standard' space - vs. complex, custom software, not to mention
the superior performance of hardware solutions).

And finally, at least in one common incarnation it's not *real*
'fault-tolerance' but just another approach to high-availability, since
unless every output is checked against an actively-executing (not just
ready-to-take-over) pair-process before being committed it won't protect
against undetected hardware failure.

- bill





