sword7@speakeasy.org wrote:
> 
> Hello folks:
> 
> Well, I finally discovered that I had not loaded 0x300 into SAVPSL before
> I execute the console program (20040000) after I endlessly debugged my
> emulator due to lack of KA630 manuals for three days.  When it was done,
> all boot problems instantly disappeared!!  Booting finally returned back
> to normal.  That's why SAVPSL must have 0x300 or boot will not work.

I would expect without *knowing* what the ROM code does that the initial
value for SAVPSL would be 0x1f0000. 0x300 is an illegal PSL value as the
'one' bits will be in a MBZ field if copied to the PSL. 0x1f0000 is
IPL 31, which will lock out all interrupts, including power fail.

> 
> Does anyone have information about three processor registers about
> SAVPSL, SAVPC, and SAVISP?  How about Q-bus specs for MicroVAX II

These three registers are unique to the MicroVAX II chip. They are
processor internal registers with addresses of:

   41   Console Saved ISP            SAVISP   R/W
   42   Console Saved PC             SAVPC    R/W
   43   Console Saved PSL            SAVPSL   R/W

The register numbers are decimal. The M[TF}PR instruction is used to
access these registers.

The Q22-Bus memory space is addressed through physical addresses
0x30000000 to 0x303fffff. Note that the Q22-Bus does not have to
have any physical memory installed to function. But if Q22-Bus
physical memory is available it is addressed via these addresses.

The Q22-Bus IO mapping register array is located at 0x2008xxxx.
Each register maps a 512 byte page of Q22-Bus memory into
a selected page of local memory. The map register contains:

  <31:31>  V        Valid bit. Indicates <14:00> value is valid.
  <30:15>           unused. These bits always read as zero.
  <14:00>  A23-A09  Address bits. These are the high 15 bits of a 24
                    bit local (physical) memory address.

The Q22-Bus IOPAGE addresses are directly addressed in the physical
address scheme at 0x20000000 to 0x20001fff.

The console ROM code must map enough local memory space to boot
the system. The Q22-Bus devices will be doing DMA into the physical
Q22-Bus address space. The mapping array will cause the DMA to
be to physical MicroVAX memory, page by page.

