In comp.os.vms Hoff Hoffman <hoffman@xdelta.zko.dec.nospam> wrote:
> In article <toqica73c3vv0c@corp.supernews.com>, sword7@speakeasy.org writes:

> :...I tried different settings in CPMBX and see
> :what happens...  I learned that I must load hex 20 into CPMBX to display
> :English properly.  If I did not load hex 20, I got ?04 message error
> :instead of "Failure." after "PC = 20040C9E" was printed. 

>   The format of the KA630 CPMBX is in module UV2DEF in library LIB.

Ok, thank you for information.  I will look into that.

Well, it seems fine now.  I thought that I have some problems with 
booting.  I studied my traced executions (in debug.log) from my emulator 
and finally understand them. (Around location 20040758).  I tested halt
actions in CPMBX and compared traced executions in 20040758.  It 
resulted different each halt action value.  Best value is 0x22 or 0x26 in 
CPMBX to boot normally from turned system on.  I resolved all problems.

In BDR step B and A, I learned that VMB was trying access Q-22 bus area.
In step B, VMB initialized Q-22 map registers, etc.  in step A, VMB
is scanning Q-22 address I/O space like device registers, etc.
I noticed that 30000000 and up are Q-22 address I/O space.  My VAX
architecture handbook failed to mention that in MicroVAX II system map.
VMB tried to access Q-22 memory area at starting 30000000 but found
non-existant memory.  It gave up and just switched to run-mode address
and jump itself (endless loop) forever...  (20051C95 JMP @#20051C95)

Well, I will try working on Q-22 bus system..  However, I does not have 
Q-22 specs for MicroVAX II. :-(

Thank you for information and help.

-- Tim Stark

