young_r@encompasserve.org (Rob Young) writes:

> 	Nick, there is the whole matter of history here.  VMS engineering
> 	beat their own internal deadline for the VAX->Alpha port by about
> 	a year.  I've heard firsthand that they have some of the finest
> 	software engineering techniques around.  Another fellow very
> 	familiar with VAX->Alpha migration described it as "a piece of cake."
> 
> 	Perhaps you can go on about such scary subjects but the folks involved
> 	most likely aren't shaking in their boots.

With respect, "because we've been ok up till now, we will be ok in
future" isn't a wise philosophy in my opinion (that's my paraphrase,
not a quote).

Alpha was engineered from the ground up to support high performance
and near-perfect VAX/VMS compatibility (e.g. those VAX float formats,
PALcode etc).

IPF hasn't been designed to support any VAX or Alpha
peculiarities. Instead it has all of the Intel, Apollo and HP-PA
peculiarities and a bunch of new ones of its own.

I think the magnitude of the task is larger, and the strength of the
company behind it severely diminished. If I'm right, it is at least
an open question whether the Alpha->IPF port will be anything like as
successful.

I'm reminded of the Ada95 issue. Back in '94 the big topic on the ada
newsgroup was "DEC Ada why is it so good". DEC produced the best Ada83
compiler bar none. This was another success for their indubitably fine
software engineering. Their Ada users were thus quite taken aback when
they just punted and licensed other Ada95 compilers (Rational and then
GNU Ada as I remember).

How do you know the Alpha/VMS crowd will not get the same shock? You
don't. Nobody does. A lot of work is needed and will have to be paid
for. Those brave folks, ready to do the job and all, will simply do
some other job at the flick of a bean-counters quill if he so
decides. Or they may pull off another triumph. Let's hope so.

Cheers,

Chris

-- 
Chris Morgan


