Chris Morgan wrote:
> I think the magnitude of the task is larger, and the strength of the
> company behind it severely diminished. If I'm right, it is at least
> an open question whether the Alpha->IPF port will be anything like as
> successful.

From a technical point of view, I have no problems believing that the VMS
engineers will be able to get VMS to run on IA64 with all of the basic
expected features, including cross-platform clustering.

Where I think VMS will suffer in in high end fancy stuff that nobody else has
(galaxies for instance).  Since neither NT nor Tru64 have these features, it
will be an uphill battle for the VMS folks to convince the wintel folks to
build boxes that support those unique VMS features. (this isn't so much chip
dependant , but because of the fact that the PC people will be in charge of
building boxes that are far more complex than the glorified desktops installed
in a 19" rack that they currently build.)

I would be more comfortable if VMS and Tandem sides worked together and built
their own boxes. If you're going to relegate VMS to only a very few focused
market niches at the high end (eg: cash cow), the model is nearly identical to
Tandem's and to take advantage of all those fancy features, you need
specialised hardware that the wintel weenies don't need (or know how to build).

On the other hand, by forcing VMS to lower itself to wintel levels of
hardware, some can argue that VMS will run on commodity hardware and thus be
more competitive.

However, the problem I see is that on the wintel el-cheapo hardware, VMS may
not be good enough to please those few large remaining customers, and Compaq's
total lack of marketing and its unwillingness to pit VMS against Windows won't
make VMS realise its potential as an OS that can work from desktop to data-centre.


