I don't think the ability to run VMS on el-cheapo hardware is a danger
by itself. On the contrary, this can even be good to make it more wide-
spread. The danger lies in the fact that people (and not just bean
counters) will do exactly what they do with Wintel-crap compared to more
expensive Wintel boxes.

"Real" customers will buy the well engineered stuff. But some will buy
the cheapest possible box, maybe not even really certified. Something
will be slightly incompatible or break prematurely, and then they blame
it on VMS. "This does not work 7/24 as advertised". Since bad rumour
spreads fast, pleople will not think "cheap bastards" but "bad software".

If you can make it clear that running it on non-certified hardware is
your own risk, then the whole thing could work. Somehow this is a job
for the marketing folks... At home I run VMS on a PWS500a converted to
au with cheap used PC parts. But I'd never ask the Q for support on
that hack :-)

Wolfgang Rupp



