I'm not quite sure how this "DG" reference got worked in here - it's not
clear to me how or what DG ever provided for JSTARS.  The JSTARS program has
been using a combination of militarized VAX servers and Alpha (Turbochannel
based) VMS systems since the early 90's.  The FIRST prototype of the plane,
with the above configuration, flew in Desert Storm.  The news release about
plane delivery was about the first UPGRADED version of this aircraft to be
delivered, which runs Alpha ES40's instead of the the VAX and older Alphas.

I do know something about JSTARS, and Northrop/Gumman Melbourne.  They are
"my" dimond support customer, and I have worked with them since before Alpha
V1.0.  I have the patches, the pewter plane, have been on the plane, in the
simulator, and have friends in NG and in the Melbourne support office.

The thing about the military is that they tend to buy a specific
configuration, and spares - for the lifetime of its mission.  Upgrading that
configuration (except perhaps minor details) tends to be a complete
project - either software changes or hardware.  Want to know how many
VAX/VMS systems defend this country today?  Can't tell you, except they're
out there.

>Also, how does DII-COE work with regards to VMS on Alpha. Hasn't Compaq
>enssentially already announced the death of VMS on Alpha that will happen
in
>about 10 years when support is pulled ?

DII/COE certifies a specific hardware and software configuration.  I believe
that there are contractual obligations to support the hw/sw for a specific
(long) period of time.  COE certification is not "broad", that is, it
doesn't extend to hardware not certified, or new versions of the software
(except as provided for by patches as appropriate).

There are people still happily using Alpha systems built with EV4's and
Turbochannels.  They may have a hard time "upgrading" the hardware, but we
(VMS) still support these systems, even though they were built a decade ago.
VMS has been traditionally slow to retire support for hardware.







