In article <9mjdfv$na8$1@pyrite.mv.net>, "Bill Todd" <billtodd@foo.mv.com> writes:

:Are you by any chance confusing Galaxy support with hard-partitioning
:support?  IIRC Gaxaxy involves the *dynamic* migration of resources among
:(and some concurrent sharing of resources by) multiple systems, and could
:(though may not - I don't know) require hardware features that simple
:hard-partitioning does not.

  Adaptive Partioning Multi-Processing (APMP) -- the OpenVMS implementation
  of APMP is known as OpenVMS Galaxy -- does require a sufficient number
  of appropriate I/O widgets for the correct operation of each instance 
  within the OpenVMS Galaxy -- the required I/O controllers, memory, CPU(s) 
  and a console must be available to each instance -- but OpenVMS Galaxy
  does not particularly require any hardware features beyond the minimum 
  requirements for widgets.

  This is how we got OpenVMS Galaxy to work on an AlphaServer 8400 system, 
  and did not require particular hardware changes beyond the installation 
  of the additional (required) KFE72 consoles and the installation of the
  minimum SRM console and OpenVMS versions.

  We have also publicly demonstrated mixed operating systems -- a Linux 
  system instance was swapping processors with an OpenVMS instance at a 
  DECUS demonstration some time ago.

