It said the top job-cutters got an average increase in salary and bonus of nearly 20
percent last year, compared with average raises for U.S. wage earners of around 3
percent and 4-percent increases for salaried employees.

Sarah Anderson, director of the global economy program at the Washington-based
Institute for Policy Studies, said it was galling ''especially in this period of
economic downturn as people are feeling very insecure about their jobs, to see that
the guys at the top have cushioned themselves.''

