In article <9mgp89$4ja$1@pegasus.csx.cam.ac.uk>, nmm1@cus.cam.ac.uk (Nick Maclaren) writes:

  OpenVMS VAX does not use the same source code pool as OpenVMS Alpha.
  (Various components do use common code across OpenVMS VAX and OpenVMS 
  Alpha, of course, but there are two seperate source code pools in use.)

  We provided a no-broken-code statement on OpenVMS VAX starting with the
  OpenVMS VAX V6.0 release, and this and the lack of certain underlying 
  hardware support both preclude various kernel-mode changes.  So yes, 
  there are cases where you can get stuck without a feature when you are 
  using the OpenVMS VAX platform -- the salient example here is Java, as 
  Java mandates IEEE floating point support and VAX does not have that.

  The problem reporting and problem tracking system is common across the
  two (and soon to be three) platforms, and across a variety of layered 
  products.

  I expect to use the same operating system source code files for both 
  OpenVMS Alpha and OpenVMS IPF, and folks here expect to make available 
  support for the next-generation OpenVMS Alpha systems, for the DII COE 
  work, and other projects -- not just the same tools and common code, 
  but the same source code pool as is planned for use with OpenVMS IPF.
  (I certainly plan to use the same code base for the source code control
  system itself, regardless of the target platform.)

  The target for the port is source compatibility, meaning that the
  application source code can be hauled across and rebuilt -- we will 
  want that for ourselves, obviously.  (Though the operating system
  kernel code itself does tend to be rather less portable and rather 
  more dependent on the underlying hardware than does application code 
  using documented interfaces.  This means that the operating system 
  kernel itself will have some conditional code and some code modules
  that are platform-specific.)

  If there are specific compatibility concerns, I can (attempt) to 
  address them here (or off-line).

