>Is it anything like:

>http://www.ibiblio.org/pub/academic/computer-science/history/pdp-11/rsx/decus/rsx80a/310115/petal.for

Oh wow.  In my CMU days it was called petal.  (It originally wandered
in from Stanford as a SAIL program for the PDP-10 and displayed on
ARDS terminals.)  Soon after I got to DEC in 1974, I fell in with the
RT-11 crowd and threw together a petal hack in Fortran, I think in
time for a DECUS event.

There's a very good chance that program is mine, probably with a few
changes after I turned my attention to porting CMU's assembler code.
That would display up to 2880 vectors.  While the fortran code only does
720, it do modulo 2880 arithmetic to keep big numbers from cos().

Very good chance I wrote most of that.  Sorry about the lack of comments.

Once I ported my CMU Petal program, I figured it was too late to rename
the fortran hack, so I renamed the assembler program Roses.

I recently wrote a version in Python as my first semi-real Python program.
I've done a couple in spreadsheets too!

