In article <9ltspb$7fn$1@dns3.cae.ca>, "Eric Everton" <Eric.Everton@CAE.com> writes:

:I am looking for some documentation/explanation of MAP registers and how
:they are used...  I am modifying a driver and the system keeps crashing
:whenever it hits the DEC Macro LOADUBA.  I am not sure I completely
:understand what it is doing.

  A map register is used to identify where in system memory a direct
  memory access (DMA) I/O transfer is reading or writing.  The map
  register makes this system memory accessable to devices on the I/O 
  bus via the I/O bus address space.  Most I/O devices are thankfully 
  blissfully unaware of host memory management nor the host page tables 
  (which implement virtual memory), the devices simply know how to read 
  and write from ranges of physical memory -- not virtual memory.  Map 
  registers make ranges of user virtual memory appear to be physically 
  contiguous, and in I/O space memory that is accessable to the particular 
  I/O device.

  If the registers are misloaded, you can read or (worse) write random 
  physical memory.  (From personal experience, an off-by-one error in 
  this area can be difficult to find, and can have some rather interesting 
  effects on overall system stability. :-)

  PDF versions of the _OpenVMS VAX Device Support Manual_ and the _OpenVMS 
  VAX Device Support Reference Manual_ are available in the archived area
  of the OpenVMS documentation website:

    http://www.openvms.compaq.com/doc/

  You may also end up having to scrounge up a copy of EK-KA630-UG, the
  CPU user's guide for the MicroVAX II processor module.  (I do not know
  if this technical material is still available for ordering.)

