Despite what you may have heard from both the mindless Intel bashers and the
well-meaning CPU evaluators like the emulators.com guy, the Intel Pentium 4
is currently the fastest CPU in the world. Sure, many things take three
times the number of cycles on the P4 as they do on the Athlon. But the P4
was designed specifically to run at ridiculous frequencies. It's a tradeoff;
the P4 is way less efficient, but way more scalable.

Unfortunately, a bunch of things stand in its way. First, most off-the-shelf
software runs like crap on the P4, simply because it was compiled and
optimized for a completely different architecture. Second, compilers aren't
yet smart enough to optimize for the P4, and it's not clear whether they
ever will be. Third, the P4 requires insane memory bandwidth, which today is
best served (and not very well) by Rambus RDRAM, which is a total ripoff.




