I'm NOT familiar with this particular model, and I don't have its documentation.

Your best bet would be the service-level docs for this system, but those
may not be readily available to you.


I think all alpha-based systems are similar in the duties of the SROM
code.  SROM is loaded into the CPU's instruction cache upon power-up.  The
I-cache is as small as 8kB, so the SROM code can't do much. The code
initially assumes NOTHING is available except the on-chip registers and
the code itself.  Even the processor-memory path is not tested and
initialized yet.

On DEC 3000 systems, for which I happen to have the docs, the SROM code
can't even talk to the console.  It reports problems via LED patterns. 
Some systems use beep codes as well as LEDs.  The cpu can support a
"mini-console" through 2 pins (not normally connected to the outside
world), and this console can be used to do low-level tests when all else
fails.  The mini-console is really for testing during manufacture, and
docs were never available outside Digital/Compaq AFAIK.  (The alpha
development boards had mini-console docs available, but I don't know how
similar the production systems were.)

On DEC 3000, SROM does things like find out the size of memory, run
various memory tests, run cache tests, and then load, decompress, and
verify the main console ROMs.  At each stage, the code changes the LED
code.  If something fails, the LED code tells how far it got.  If
everything works, control is passed to the console code that was loaded
from ROM.

If SROM code can't find enough good memory, or finds problems with the ROM
contents, it will get very angry and halt.  Two symptoms in this area
might be checked without specialized info and/or equipment:

You can remove, reseat, swap the memory modules, and see if the symptoms
change.  You'll need to know what constitutes a "set" of memory modules in
this system; a set might be pairs, quads, or whatever group of modules. 
You might find a bad memory module, or reseating everything might fix the
system.

If the console ROMs are scrambled, you might improve things by
re-programming the ROMs.  Newer alphas have the "fail-safe loader", which
I believe is a mechanism to re-program the ROMs even if they are
completely wiped out.  I guess the SROM code is smart enough to take care
of this task.  I've never done it, and don't know the details.  I think
the VMS FAQ has pointers.

Any other problems are likely related to sick hardware that's permanently
attached to the motherboard, or the SROM itself is sick.  For this sort of
thing, you likely need a Real Computer Repairman, who knows about the
system in question.  In practice, you need Compaq to fix it for you.

