Any processor with 256 KB of cache and the P4's performance level is
going to consume a lot of memory bandwidth. The only thing close are
the EV6, the PA-8700 (integer), and Itanium (FP) and these sport at
least 2 MB of cache. The P4's small cache is exacerbated by the 128
byte line size which increases capacity misses compared to say PIII
coppermine which has 256 KB of cache and 32 byte line size.

The Northwood P4 (0.13 um) has 512 KB of on-chip cache and this will
reduce memory traffic by at least 30% on average for a given level
of performance. Northwood will support an increase in system interface
bandwidth by 33% when a second generation chipset becomes available.
Thus Northwood will have less memory constriction than a 2 GHz
Willamette until it reaches about 3.8 GHz.


