It is possible - we've managed to change the IP address in two of them
a couple of times. It has proven to be tricky - not all of the methods
given in the manual actually work every time in my experience.

"What manual" you ask? That would be the Declaser 5000 Series Ethernet
Card User's Guide. The old Digital order number is EK-D5100-UG.

The listed methods use RARP, ping, nsconfig, and you may be able to do
it through one or both of the Netware PCONSOLE and/or EtherTalk NPmanage
tools on the relevant type of system.

I'm not certain how we managed it, but I think that the last time it was
done by connecting a PC directly to it (rather than going through our
switch - nothing seemed to work going through the switch even though it
really should have) using the ping method.

The ping method:

1) Add an entry to your ARP cache specifying the desired IP address and
 the printer's ethernet hardware address.
2) Power up the printer.
3) Ping the IP address you specified in step 1.

At this point the printer is supposed to read the IP address in the ping
packet (which is delivered to it, and which it recognizes, because the
packet has its ethernet address) and reset its IP address.

4) Turn printer off.
5) Turn printer on.

The configuration sheet for the network card should now show the new
IP address and it should also now respond to a ping of that address.

--- Carl


