In my understanding, it all depends on what you mean by 'optimize.' In my
experience with Tru64 and linux on axp, it would be a stretch to say that
gcc excells at that task, at least when compared with the compaq
compilers. However, the binaries produced by gcc aren't bad - they just
aren't that good. I have absolutely no idea how well the MS VC axp
compiler performs. I suspect that none of this is particularly relevant,
though. If you're running NT on your alphas, then the compiler vs compiler
performance differences aren't likely to be anything more than you're
already suffering by using NT. Use gcc, or VC axp if you can get your
hands on it.

