"Michael L. Umbricht" wrote:

> BTW, does anyone know if there are any exceptions to the DEC 2-5-2
> part number scheme:
> 
> ##-#####-#B     DECsystem-10 system software
> ##-#####-#D     DECsystem-10/20 diagnostic software
> ##-#####-#M     DECsystem-20 system software
> 
> For instance, RSX20F TOOLS is BB-M403A-BK or BB-AE97B-BH for
> TOPS-20 KL-B UPD5 (could someone explain the -BH for me?)
> 
> You see, we need to sort through a very large quantity of DEC labeled
> media and pull the 36-bit distributions out of the pdp-11, VAX, etc pile.
> I'd like to send my contact a simple set of rules, as to what to look
> for to make sure we save every piece of DECsystem related media.
> Some of the media is archived off-site, and the company that pulls
> the tapes will have no clue how to decode the labels.

The full scheme is 2-5-4-2, and was (invented?) and maintained by the
late Dick Best Sr., the Chief Engineer of DEC.  Example:

http://groups.google.com/groups?selm=3A33FC47.5FA347%40bellatlantic.net

Hardware codes for attributes like ``50 Hz'' would be reused for
something completely different for software.  The scheme was allegedly
the bane of many in manufacturing, but was never replaced because none
of their suggested alternatives satisfied all of the requirements
fulfilled by the existing scheme.  So there might be a rule set you
could use, but there might not.

The best you'll probably be able to do is transcribing or scanning the
archive's receipts for Sikorsky's submissions and letting net volunteers
scan them.
				Good luck,
				/AHM
-- 
Alan Howard Martin				AMartin@MA.UltraNet.Com


