In article <eCgf7.45995$We3.623677@news.easynews.com>, "Chris Czerlinsky" <chriscz@yahoo.com> writes:
>
>First, I appologize for asking such a stupid question, but here goes....
>
As has been said before, there are no stupid questions.

>We inherited a 2100 4/200 with NT4.0 installed.  We saw it booted and
>working at a customer site, but after bringing it back to our office and
>trying to boot up, it appears that the system can no longer locate the NT
>directory.  I don't know if someone intentionally erased the boot selection
>(from the 'setup the system' menu) or what, and it turns out NT wasn't
>installed in the default /os/winnt directory.
>
>My question, is there a way to find out where NT resides?  Something I can
>do from the SRC menu?  I talked to our compaq hardware person, and she said
>that she didn't know if there is a way to find the directory info.

Did you ask the former owner if they did anything to the system, such
as erasing the disk to protect their data before giving up the machine
(assuming this is part of whatever agreement the two of you had).

I'm not sure what you mean by SRC menu: do you mean the SRM console?
If so, the answer is probably no, SRM doesn't know anything about
the Windows disk structure.  You need to be in the ARC console to
get anywhere with NT.

I have not tried this on this particular machine: but in the past,
on a number of NT systems which have had problems, I have been
successful in using NT's recovery procedure.  Basically, you
start off doing an installation of NT (but do NOT initialize any
of your disks!).  After the installation procedure finds all of
your devices, you should be presented with a menu of options.  The
options should include doing a new install of NT, or looking for
(Rescuing) an existing version of NT.  Hopefully, this option
will find your NT system for you: it has worked for me in the
past.

If that doesn't work, and there is something on the existing disks
you need, I would suggest plugging in another disk (your 2100 should
have a built-in StorageWorks shelf) and do a clean install of NT
to that disk.  You can do just a minimal installation.  Once you
boot that system, it should be able to mount the old system disk
and you can look at it.  Or, if the disk with the system on it
you wish to recover is removeable, you can take it over to another
NT system (it can even be an Intel-based system) and look at it
there.  You should be able to find the various directories and
files with just the normal NT tools.

-- 
