:hoffman@xdelta.zko.dec.nospam (Hoff Hoffman) writes:
:> : ? TPC      0000.4001
:> :      FFFFFF05 FFFFFF05 FFFFFF05 FFFFFF05 FFFFFF05 FFFFFF05 FFFFFF05 FFFFFF03
:> 
:>   That's the tape controller, and the diagnostics didn't like it...
:
:No. It has MicroVMS 4.something installed,...

  So?

  If the console doesn't like "it", then the operating system probably also
  won't like "it".  

  You must get the console self-tests to work first, then you can (and 
  probably should) try to load (more recent) OpenVMS VAX and try to use 
  the system. 

  Most VAX and Alpha systems learn about the configuration from the
  console -- and on more recent systems, this design becomes more central,
  as OpenVMS learns more (or all) about the system and I/O configuration 
  from the console.   OpenVMS Galaxy is centrally dependent on the system
  configuration information that is provided to each instance by the SRM
  console.

:"show dev" didn't show anything resembling a tape so the about only thing 
:I could do was trying to change controller address, no success. 

  MicroVAX 2000 and VAXstation 2000 systems do not themselves particularly 
  have I/O controller addresses.  Messing with addresses on the almost-SCSI 
  bus on the MicroVAX 2000 will get you in trouble -- it's not SCSI, it's 
  "almost-SCSI".  With the almost-SCSI, peripheral devices are expected to 
  be at known addresses, and SCSI devices are not particularly known to work.

"I also tried hooking that tape drive
:to a NCR scsi card sitting in linux-box but it didn't find anything: 
:is the tape dead or is the bus really a non-scsi-bus and a scsi-adapter
:really is supposed not to find anything?

  The MicroVAX 2000 does not have SCSI.

  If the device is the SCSI variant of the TK, then you have a dead device 
  or a bad Linux software or hardware configuration.  If the device is the 
  almost-SCSI variant, then Linux and a SCSI connection may or may not work 
  with it.

:> :   SYS      0000.0001
:> :?? NI       0000.7004  V1.2
:> 
:>   Network.
:
:Yes, if I remove ethernet cable, it doesn't boot right away but gives
:me the >>> -prompt .. how to disable autoboot, btw?

  IIRC, the relevent commands (specific to the MicroVAX 2000 and the
  VAXstation 2000 series) are:

    TEST 51

    TEST 52

    TEST 53

  The former sets the default boot device (blank is sniffer, specified
  device otherwise), the middle sets the R5 (VMB) value (see the FAQ), 
  and the latter sets the power-up behaviour code.  Codes: 1 is restart, 
  2 is reboot, 3 is halt.

  EK-ZNAGG-GZ is the VAXstation 2000 Hardware Information docs.

:>   You must have the TK50Z-F*, and not the TK50Z-G* drive.
:> 
:>   The TK50Z-F* (usually -FA) must be at bus address one.
:
:TK50Z-F3 says label in the back. 
:
:>   You do not have a SCSI bus on this system.
:
:So TK50Z-F3 is not a SCSI device?

  The bus is almost-SCSI, not SCSI.  The TK50Z-F* has the ROM code for
  the MicroVAX 2000 and VAXstation 2000 series almost-SCSI adapter.

  There have been various previous discussions of the almost-SCSI bus
  found on the VAXstation 2000 and MicroVAX 2000 series.


