Sam,

As a rule, SCSI is not built into a "normal" Intel-based Pentium or
later motherboard.  There are some exceptions, usually boards designed
for server or high-end "workstation" use.  Depending on the vintage of
the motherboard, built-in SCSI connectors can be 50-, 68-, or 80-pin,
sometimes with two of the three.  the IBM Intellistation I am using
right now has one 50-pin and (2) 68-pin connectors on the motherboard.

You'll need to double-check the specs of the 80-pin drives versus the
specs of your Alpha.  And there are the typical SCSI considerations of
termination, unique SCSI IDs (or SCAM), SCSI bus cable length, and
total impedence on the SCSI bus.

Except for occasional firmware mods to identify a SCSI unit as "DEC",
the SCSI stuff used on Alphas is industry-standard.  Some of the
earlier DEC boxes absolutely require some of their SCSI devices to
identify themselves as "DEC", which does not preclude their being used
in non-DEC systems, BTW... Ben Myers

