What applications will be ported to Tru64 on Alpha in the next several years
and what support is Compaq providing ISVs in these efforts?  If I have an
application that might be ported to Tru64, what incentives does Compaq
offer?

What resources will be available to ISVs to support porting and testing
applications on IA64?  How do ISVs apply for this support?

Current Tru64 clusters support Memory Channel; will Memory Channel be
supported on IA64 and will mixed Alpha and IA64 clusters be supported?

Have the plans for Alpha HPTC systems changed?  How?  Will Quadrics be
supported with IA64 systems?

When will 64 (and greater) CPU SMP IA64 systems be available?

Approximately half the IA64 instructions are SIMD (Single Instruction,
Multiple Data); when will compiler and/or library support for these
instructions be available?

Over two thirds of the instructions of an IPF chip are IA32; will Tru64
support execution of IA32 unix programs using the IA32 hardware execution
mode?

Compaq produced a convincing paper that explained why Alpha would exceed the
performance of IS64 for many years to come.  Alpha roadmaps emphasized SMP
scalability of EV7 the SMT capabilities of EV8, indicating that they offered
more realistic opportunities for high performance well into the future.
These are the same strategies being pursued byIBM in its Power4
implementation.  What has changed in the past few months that invalidate the
argument that Alpha would be competitive with IA64 well into the future?
Why isn't a switch to IBM's Power4 in the next year a better strategy than
staying with Alpha and waiting for an uncertain level of performance with
IA64?

The IA64 instruction set is very complex with many instruction forms;
current compiler technology does not seem to be able to convert non-floating
point code to a form that exploits the complexity of the architecture.  This
suggests that IA64 will need to invest in significant logic to spped up
cycle time and at the same time invest in out of order execution.  This will
lead to larger chips and more power consumption, keeping the cost of systems
up, or driving them higher.  Do you have information that refutes this
reasoning and when will if be presented to the public?






