Ben,

As a long time resident of the District of Columbia, I am happy to report
that DC uses the excellent Datavote system wherein the Hollerith card is the
ballot. The card has the voter choices clearly printed on a solid card.
Voters insert the card in a machine that allow the voter to align a
cutting-lever directly against the candidate or referendum for which they
wish to vote. Pressing the level down punches a clean square hole through
the solid card. Because the Datavote card has the candidates and referendums
printed clearly on the card, and the machine creates a very clean cut
through the solid card, the voter can immediately examine the card and see
for whom or what they did indeed vote. The Datavote system is as good as, if
not better than, the much vaulted optical scanning technology. Datavote has
served DC well for many years and I see no reason to replace it.

This is in contrast to the inferior Votomatic system used in some Florida
counties where the so called butterfly ballot is a piece of paper laid over
the partially pre-punched Hollerith card. The voter uses a pin to press down
through the paper ballot and hopefully will completely remove the
appropriate partially pre-punched chad. Since the Hollerith card is not the
complete ballot, and has no useful information printed on it, the voter
cannot easily examine the card and ascertain that they have indeed voted for
whom and what the intended. Votomatic systems are very cheap and affordable,
but unreliable. The good news is that counties and replace Votomatic with
Datavote and still retain the investment in the centralized card reading
equipment.

If you or anyone else is interested in the facts behind the information
processing technology used in the last election, I can suggest some URLs you
can visit.

Sincerely,

John Fredrickson

