>ballot. The card has the voter choices clearly printed on a solid card.
>Voters insert the card in a machine that allow the voter to align a
>cutting-lever directly against the candidate or referendum for which they
>wish to vote. Pressing the level down punches a clean square hole through
>the solid card. Because the Datavote card has the candidates and referendums
>printed clearly on the card, and the machine creates a very clean cut
>through the solid card, the voter can immediately examine the card and see
>for whom or what they did indeed vote. The Datavote system is as good as, if
>not better than, the much vaulted optical scanning technology. Datavote has
>served DC well for many years and I see no reason to replace it.
>
