Hey Arne!
YOur original response to the question was on the money, why bother?
One of the things that customers have been taught by various vendors,
in an attempt to manage their expectations, is that all you have to
do is reboot every once in a while, say once a week. With customers
trained this way, why bother?

In days of old we had this theory of flogiston, that is tied to the
concept of flooby dust.  Flooby dust fills up the cache in suns,
so the cache needs to be recycled.  In MS boxes, it fills up the
bit bucket and the memory starts to leak. So, you shut it off and =

let the flooby dust settle to the bottom of the bit bucket, then
restart.  Real systems run a bit longer than MS or E10Ks.  I do
like the ultrasparc boxes, not bad terminals.

