Hi,  setup follows, with question at the end:

I'm salvaging a pallet of surplus uVAXen 3100's, Storage Expansion, an
InfoServer, some DECstations (now mostly parts, where applicable  --
i.e. drives), and some VT1300s.  This is hobbyist; it happened largely
due to a little creativity in rearranging a crowded basement and an
understanding wife.

Had pretty good luck -- I've got 3 or 4 working so far.  Today, I
tried to salvage an ex-NASA VAXstation 3100 M38.  Pretty clean, only a
little corrosion around the battery (leaked), but cleaned up.

First boot got to the dead sargeant ">>>", and SHOW DEV worked
alright, but failed to boot.  ("Darn, no interesting NASA data on that
one...")  Powered off.  Attached CD-ROM (untested RRD-42) to try and
boot Hobbyist VMS CD-ROM.  Funny power-up test (below.)  Tried known
good Toshiba CD-ROM drive.  Tried no SCSI drive.  Tried with and
without 4MB memory expansion (suspected that it might have experienced
ESD death when removed for cleaning.)  Dead backup battery on/off. 
Visual inspection -- everything seems OK, but now boot sequence goes
like this:

F..E..D..C..B..{insert 3-5 minute pause here but not new line}
F..E..D..C..B..A..9..8..7..6..5..4..3..2..1

? F 	0101  0101.0101
? E	0101  0101.0101
<snip, but run through all 15 power power up tests with same code>
? 1     0101  0101.0101

**QUESTION**
Is anyone able to pass along the significance of this error code, and
if so, please do.  This machine acts like all the good uVAXen I have
until it gets to ..B {long pause}.  I know this is the MEMory test, so
I suspect that's the culprit.  No, I haven't tested with other memory
yet; I'm not willing to give up the good (scarce) memory in the
working machines in case something here might be bringing it down.

My suspicion is that this is a "global" hardware error of some sort,
'cause it's a little too orderly to be random and the serial port
bothers to tell it to the terminal.

Can anyone confirm or deny, and any information is appreaciated.

Thanks in advance.

Phil Mendelsohn


