Robert DiRosario wrote:
> 
> The only difference between a VAXStation 3100 and a MicroVAX 3100 is the
> VAXStation has a frame buffer (video)  board.  Remove the frame buffer, or
> disable it with the switch on the rear of the system, and it becomes a
> MicroVAX.

The MicroVAX 3100 (Model 10) and the 
VAXstation 3100 (Model 30) are not the 
same apart from a frame buffer!

You can, however, make the VAXstation think
it is a VAXserver by flicking the switch.
You cannot (IIRC) remove the mono frame buffer
built into the motherboard (although you
can remove an addon GPX or SPX).

All of this is moot since he almost certainly
has bad memory.

Try pulling the memory and reseating 
(if you happen to have two memory
boards stacked one on top of the
other, try just using one). This
should at least tell you whether
only one of the boards is bad.

It is pretty unlikely that swapping
in known good memory will result in
harm to that known good memory. It is
not impossible, but I've never had 
it happen to me. (Which will be of
no comfort to you if things 
go wrong :0 )

You could try running with all the 
memory pulled. I don't know whether it will
get very far ... but if it still reproduces
the above symptoms for the first few tests
then you probably have an issue with the
motherboard.

Antonio


