Capellas's statement is a classic example of Clintonesque meaning
mangling.  It appears to say
that the customer response is so positive that the company can hardly
believe it - but what it
really says is that the listener should not believe that the response
was as positive as the speaker is insinuating.   Given the real (and
well deserved) level of customer confidence in the veracity of Compaq
management's statements (zero) a more compelling statement would have
been "we spoke to the
CIOs at our 100 largest customers and ##% of them said they were in
favor of the change."  But they
can't say that, because they probably only went down a very short list
until they found somebody who said something vaguely positive, like
"Well, at least Intel knows how to market its products, so if you use
their CPU,  maybe the VMS group will be able to survive your moronic
management long enough to be bought by somebody who knows what they're
doing."

Which to Q management instantly became "unbelievably positive".

