We've got an existing WAN in star configuration over frame relay,
RouteAbout Access EWs (DRS V3.0-1) at each location running bridging
(for LAT, all NetBIOS filters enabled) and routing (for IP).  The
remote sites consist of the routeabout, one or two DECserver 90Ms, and
between 1 and 8 peecees, which are set up for IP only.  Links are 56K
and there is no internet access, browsing, or peecee-style file
sharing or networking across the links (only Telnet from powerterm to
access an Alpha VMS system, and the DECservers).

LAT performance is lagging, and there are occasional LAT disconnects. 
The router logging process produces bridging timer messages which DNPG
diagnoses as inadequate bandwidth causing timeouts.  These occur far
more often than the LAT disconnects, which perhaps implies that most
of the interruptions are too brief to impact LAT connectivity (but not
performance). There does not appear to be any problem with the IP
routing.

There is a Bandwidth Reservation System available on these routers. 
It appears that it breaks down by protocol, and one of the listed
protocols is ASRT, though it does not specifically say that bridged
traffic can be prioritized, and I'm far enough from expert on these
routers that I can't determine that from the available docs.

Does anyone know if setting up BRS with high priority and a set
percentage of bandwidth given to ASRT protocol will in fact bump the
priority of the communications used by LAT in this arrangement?  Any
idea if that has the capability of helping with the situation we are
seeing here?

Rich Jordan


